1. `Steps to solve a problem` : 
	1. Understand the problem
	2. Input given
	3. Solution(on paper in form of flowchart and pseudocode) as after getting solution on paper 
	it will be easy to write code for that solution.
	4. code of solution figured out on paper in step 3 . 
	
	
2. `Flowchart` : Diagram of the solution 
	1. Components of a flowchart : start , exit , input/output, process , arrows,
	2. And for each steps we use these components accordingly as our flow of programs goes. And 
	for connecting each steps and showing flow of our program we use arrows. And for decision 
	component we whenever we are using any conditional statement that will give yes or no if give
	yes then usually we bring a arrow from left hand side of decision block to downside facing
	and for no from right hand side to downward facing.
	
	
3. `Pseudo code` : General logic of solution , we write in english , and this is pre step of code .
    And we can directly write this from our flowchart for that problem.
	1. `How to write` : We write this in step by wise manner in simple english language and
    there are no strict rules for pseudo code , just how our program will go and work written in
    english language in step wise manner . 
	2. `how to write decision block in pseudo code` : `Ex:` 
		1. input a & b
		2. is a < b
			  print a
			else
				print b
		3. exit 
			we can even use if at place of is for checking conditions.
	3. `how to write loop in pseudo code`: `ex :`
		1. input n
		2. count = 1 , sum = 0 
		3. while count <= n
			1. sum += count
			2. count += 1
		4. print sum
		5. exit
		
		
4. Generally when we approach problem we never write flowchart we write some pseudo code and not
    necessary to write for all problems.
    
    
5. `Dry running code` : dry running means checking our code with any example and checking whether
    working fine or not.
    
    
6. `Way to create a standard empty array of size 2` : `int cnt[2] {};` --> a has 2 indices 0 and 1 both are
    0 initialized.
    
    
7. `changing case of first character or any specific of a string` : `p[0] = toupper(p[0]);` this will uppercase
    first character `p[0] = tolower(p[0]);` this will lowercase first character of the string . 
    
    
8. `changing case of whole string ` : `transform(s.begin(),s.end(),s.begin(),[](unsigned char c){ return tolower(c) ;)};`
    this will lowercase all characters and to uppercase all characters just replace tolower with toupper . And this will
    change your original string . If you want to leave any starting characters unchanged then just do +1 with begin()
    `transform(s.begin()+1,s.end(),s.begin()+1,[](unsigned char c){ return tolower(c) ;)};` like this will lowercase all
    characters of string s but leave first character of s unchanged . 
    
    
9. `creating a vector of pair` : we can basically create a vector of pair means assign certain data type values with 
    another and this is especially useful when we wanted to check any parameter in sorted order and use another parameter . 
	1. `creation syntax` : `vector <pair<int,int>> d; ` created a d vector of pairs and then for taking input `for(int i = 0 ;
	i < n ; i++){ int a , b ; cin >> a >> b; d.push_back({a,b});` and how we can accesss values for comparision as `for(int i = 0 ; i < n ; i ++ )
 {if(d[i].first > variable){ variable += d[i].second;}} ` or you can use `d.emplace_back(a, b)` at place of `d.push_back({a,b})` 
 this emplace is specifically used It works for any data type you put into a vector (like custom classes, strings, or vectors of
 vectors ,pairs) and this avoids copying and then pasting trying put the value and this is much faster . 
 
